From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 08:55:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: b43: drop the A-MSDU subframes the MAC splits off

The MAC splits a received A-MSDU into its subframes, one RX buffer each,
and flags them in the RX status with the A-MSDU bit. b43 passes them to
mac80211 as complete frames. The first one carries the 802.11 header,
followed by subframe data where mac80211 expects the QoS control and the
CCMP header, so with hardware decryption mac80211 takes a random PN from
it. That PN is stored for whatever TID the garbage names, and every later
frame of that TID is dropped as a replay until the station rekeys: on an
EA6900 (BCM4360) with an ath11k client, a 5 GHz upload stopped within
minutes once the client started sending A-MSDUs.

Drop the subframes, as brcmsmac does (brcms_c_recv(), RXS_AMSDU_MASK).

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/xmit.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/xmit.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/xmit.c
@@ -1195,6 +1195,16 @@ void b43_rx(struct b43_wldev *dev, struc
 		 * the key in software. */
 		goto drop;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * The MAC splits a received A-MSDU into its subframes, one buffer
+	 * each, and flags them with the A-MSDU bit. None of them is a
+	 * complete MPDU: the first one carries the 802.11 header followed by
+	 * subframe data where the QoS control and the security header belong,
+	 * so mac80211 would read a random CCMP PN from it and take every later
+	 * frame of that TID for a replay. Drop them, as brcmsmac does.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(macstat & B43_RX_MAC_AMSDU))
+		goto drop;
 
 	/* Skip PLCP and padding */
 	padding = (macstat & B43_RX_MAC_PADDING) ? 2 : 0;
